    TXT,
    'article' => [
        'intro' => <<<'TXT'
        California sales tax works out Sales tax from Purchase amount and ZIP code. The arithmetic is the whole method: sales tax is worked out from purchase amount by one formula, applied once.
        TXT,
        'sections' => [
            ['title' => 'How the California sales tax calculator works', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>The California sales tax calculator applies one relationship to 2 numbers and reports Sales tax.</p><p>The relationship it solves reads Sales tax = get_sales_tax(Purchase amount, ZIP code).</p><p>The result panel carries Sales tax, each in a box of its own rather than buried in a sentence.</p>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'What to enter, and what comes back', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>The form asks for 2 boxes, and nothing else.</p><ul><li><strong>Purchase amount</strong> — a plain number, no unit</li><li><strong>ZIP code</strong> — a plain number, no unit</li></ul><p>What you get back is Sales tax, shown next to the form rather than on a page of its own.</p>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'Reading the answer', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>As far as Sales tax is concerned the numbers are unitless — it neither converts nor guesses a unit, so enter every value in the same scale you intend to read the answer in.</p>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'The formula, and what turns it round', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>Below is the arithmetic in full. Every symbol on the right of an equals sign is one of the boxes on the form.</p><ul><li><strong>Sales tax</strong> = <code>get_sales_tax(Purchase amount, ZIP code)</code></li></ul>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'Checking an answer by hand', 'html' => '<p>A result here can always be checked: enter it in its own box, leave a different one empty, and the page should hand the first value back.</p>'],
            ['title' => 'Where the numbers usually go wrong', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <ul><li><strong>The answer typed back in.</strong> Sales tax is a result, and each can be typed into. Putting a past answer back into one of them turns the calculation round and solves for whichever box is then empty — useful, but only if that is what you meant to do.</li><li><strong>Purchase amount left as a guess.</strong> Anything typed into Purchase amount is treated as measured. The page has no way to flag an estimate, and the answer will read exactly as confidently either way.</li><li><strong>ZIP code left as a guess.</strong> Anything typed into ZIP code is treated as measured. The page has no way to flag an estimate, and the answer will read exactly as confidently either way.</li><li><strong>Measurements mixed across scales.</strong> A calculation cannot tell a measured value from an estimated one. If Purchase amount is an estimate, the answer is only as good as that number, whatever precision the panel displays.</li></ul>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'What each box means', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>The boxes on the California sales tax form, one at a time: what each one wants, how it is measured, and what the page does if you leave it empty.</p><ul><li><strong>Purchase amount</strong> takes a plain number with no unit attached. It feeds Sales tax. Fill it from a measurement rather than a memory — Purchase amount is the kind of value that gets estimated once and then copied from page to page for years.</li><li><strong>ZIP code</strong> takes a plain number with no unit attached. It feeds Sales tax. Fill it from a measurement rather than a memory — ZIP code is the kind of value that gets estimated once and then copied from page to page for years.</li></ul><p>Every one of those boxes is optional in a sense: fill all but one and the page solves for the one you left. It is only when two are blank that the page has nothing to choose between.</p>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'What each result tells you', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>The panel reports 1 value. Here is what each one is for, and what to compare it against.</p><ul><li><strong>Sales tax</strong> is a plain number. The value follows from Purchase amount and ZIP code. Treat the first significant digit as the answer and the rest as information about precision — the last digits move when an input moves, and that is normal. It can be typed back into as well as read from, which is how the same page solves backwards when you know the answer and want the input.</li></ul>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'Getting an answer, step by step', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
